<main>
  <section class="intro" style="text-align:left;">
    <h1>Analysing verb placement</h1>
    <p>The DAKODA project explored the automatic annotation of verb order and developmental stages in learner texts of German as a second language. 
    The DAKODA infrastructure enables the exploration and analysis of annotated datasets in standardized formats (CAS) across multiple corpora.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Workflow in DAKODA</h2>
    <p>On this page, we present the fundamentals of verb order analysis in the DAKODA project. 
    Details on the individual quality evaluation procedures and technical steps can be found in the linked references.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Annotations of developmental stages in DAKODA</h2>
    <p>To annotate the DAKODA data for internal project analyses and subsequent provision in the corpus infrastructure, the following workflow was used:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Harmonizing the DAKODA database into suitable formats.</li>
      <li>Importing existing target hypotheses from the source corpora and metadata.</li>
      <li>Generating automatic target hypotheses, as well as parsing and tagging the learner texts and their associated target hypotheses. Each text was parsed both with SyntaxDot according to the DU scheme and with spaCy according to the TiGer scheme for German.</li>
      <li>Automatic, rule-based annotation of verb placement types on finite verbs.</li>
      <li>Compilation of the original corpus data (texts, metadata, and, where applicable, target hypotheses) and the DAKODA annotation layer in CAS format into XMI files.</li>
      <li>Making the various file formats available for download. More information about the individual download formats can be found <a href="use.en.html">here</a>.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Manual annotation with stage tags</h2>
    <p>To assess the automatic assignment of developmental stages, staff members of the DAKODA project annotated the different verb order types in several annotation experiments, in accordance with the DAKODA specifications developed within the project, using a sample of data from the learner corpora MERLIN (Winiewski et al. 2013) and DISKO (Wisniewski et al. 2022), followed by an assessment of inter-annotator agreement.</p>
    <p>The annotation experiments were initially designed to measure the extent to which human annotators agreed in their analysis of verb order. Since learner language is often ambiguous or underspecified, manual annotators also created minimal target hypotheses when annotating verb stages, in order to make the level of interpretation transparent on the basis of the developmental stage annotated in the learner data.</p>
    <p>Furthermore, the manual annotations served as the gold standard for assessing the quality of the methods explored.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Automatic annotation of developmental stages</h2>
    <p>The design of the automated developmental stage annotations consists of a rule-based procedure using features from automatic dependency parses to assign one or more placement types to finite verbs in the learner language data. Using the manually created rules, the data was then automatically annotated with syntactic dependencies, the parsed subclauses were assigned to the topological sentence model in a rule-based way, and, within this model, assigned to developmental stages according to the DAKODA specifications.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Annotations of learner language data</h2>
    <p>To develop an automated procedure for annotating developmental stages, several approaches were pursued during the course of the project. The fundamental challenge is that learner language data differs from target language data, which means that existing analysis tools, such as automatic parsers or POS taggers, cannot be reliably applied to learner data. Individual studies therefore often use time-consuming manual procedures for POS tagging or parsing.</p>
    <p>First, various minimal target hypotheses were generated. The target hypotheses created in DAKODA are based on different methods: the target hypothesis generated with <a href="https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/model_doc/mbart">mBART</a> is produced by an AI-based automatic translation method in which existing deviations are corrected into well-formed subclauses (GEC = Grammatical Error Correction). A second target hypothesis is generated by <a href="https://github.com/NLP2CT/TransGEC">TransGEC</a>, and a third target hypothesis is based on the Mistral model.</p>
    <p>One approach combined the outputs of several parsers (transkit, syntaxdot, stanza, udpipe 1, udpipe 2) for the learner layer in order to obtain a more robust analysis. A second approach compared the syntaxdot parse of the learner layer with the syntaxdot parse of an automatically generated target hypothesis, in particular to identify the placement type ADV (multiple constituents in the prefield).</p>
    <p>In practice, neither approach proved to be significantly better than a direct analysis of the learner layer. Nevertheless, the DAKODA data include the amalgamated parses and the generated target hypotheses for documentation purposes and to enable further analyses. The target hypotheses also constitute an orthographically correct normalization layer. Since they were annotated with regard to developmental stages using the same rule-based procedure as the learner layer, they make it possible to compare the identified verb placement between finite verb tokens in the learner layer and in the target hypothesis.</p>
    <p><strong>Note:</strong> The developmental stage annotations displayed in the DAKODA annotations and on the dashboard are the result of the exploratory process outlined above and may still contain errors.</p>
  </section>
